\section*{A limiting density threshold for every fixed random clause size}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Each fixed random clause size $k\geq3$ is claimed to have a positive finite limiting satisfiability threshold; the manuscript credits an earlier resolution by Carenini.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Fix integer \(k\ge3\). Sample independent proper \(k\)-clauses with replacement, choosing \(k\) distinct variables uniformly and each literal sign uniformly. If \(P_n(m)\) denotes the satisfiability probability, the source claims a finite positive constant \(\alpha_k\) with
\[
 \lim_{n\to\infty}P_n(\lfloor cn\rfloor)=
 \begin{cases}1,&0\le c<\alpha_k,\\0,&c>\alpha_k.\end{cases}
\]
No value at \(c=\alpha_k\), or numerical formula for \(\alpha_k\), is part of this theorem.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Replacement of shorter clauses and integrated forcing control a capped last-satisfiable index. Clause-omission pivotal estimates give concentration, while comparisons between system sizes make the normalized centers converge, producing an alternative existence proof.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Each fixed random clause size $k\geq3$ is claimed to have a positive finite limiting satisfiability threshold; the manuscript credits an earlier resolution by Carenini. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 235, the selected entries establish threshold existence, threshold computability, linear variance for $k\geq4$, and the earlier $O(n\log n)$ bound for $k=3$. The later linear three-clause variance improvement is not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{FixedClauseThreshold.lean}, \texttt{SATComputability.lean}, \texttt{SATSharpness.lean}, \texttt{SATVariance.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

